2026-02-11 01:03:51,944 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document What Is Net Present Value (NPV)?

Net present value (NPV) measures whether a project or investment will be profitable by comparing today’s value of future cash inflows to its costs. In general, projects with a positive NPV are worth undertaking, while those with a negative NPV are not. is relevant
2026-02-11 01:03:52,848 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-11 01:03:52,852 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Positive NPV vs. Negative NPV

A positive NPV indicates that the projected earnings generated by a project or investment—discounted for their present value—exceed the anticipated costs, also in today’s dollars. It is assumed that an investment with a positive NPV will be profitable.

An investment with a negative NPV will result in a net loss. This concept is the basis for the net present value rule, which says that only investments with a positive NPV should be considered. is relevant
